## ===========================================================================
## TEISHA'S WARES — the silks in the tent, and the buy verb
##
## Book 2 Part 17 Chapter 2 (`story.ni:3023-3057`): the collective display of
## silk garments, the five kinds a browser can pick out — gowns, capes,
## cloaks, shawls, robes — and the rules the source hangs on them: stealing
## refused with the friendship line, buying refused for want of money. The
## buy verb itself is the source's Part 27 (`story.ni:688-693`): a global
## action whose every other answer is "Nothing is on sale."
##
## THE TRADE IS GIVING'S ALONE — RULED (David, 2026-08-31: "'give necklace
## to teisha' should still be the action"). The source's buy-with-necklace
## redirect (`story.ni:3035`, `try trading the necklace with Teisha`) is
## NOT carried: buying the silks always answers the no-money line, and the
## TE20 trade runs only through giving or showing the necklace to Teisha
## (`npc-teisha.chord`, `trade-for-the-necklace`). The ruling supersedes
## the earlier flag-drain build that ran the trade from `buy`.
##
## READINGS:
##   1. The cloaks' `[first time]` tail — "You realize suddenly that this is
##      exactly what you need: the perfect disguise!" (`story.ni:3050`) — is
##      carried verbatim ON THE CLOAKS for now. Its placement (the remake's
##      disguise is the dress) and its calm-walk timing belong to the full
##      cloak-era text sweep David ruled into a GitHub issue (2026-08-31).
##   2. The source's shared silkware vocabulary ("silk", "silks", "garment",
##      "clothes", "fabric"…) named all five kinds at once, with a clarify
##      rule quietly picking the cloaks. Chord has no clarify rule, so the
##      shared words live on the silk garments collective instead: `x silks`
##      answers with the display, never a which-do-you-mean prompt among
##      five. Each kind keeps only its own words.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * The buy-with-necklace redirect (`story.ni:3035`) — David's ruling
##     above.
##   * `trade <silks> for <necklace>` and `buy <something> with <something>`
##     (`story.ni:818, 3038`) — a story grammar line carries one slot; the
##     trade rides giving or showing the necklace.
##   * The "does the player mean buying something when the player has the
##     noun: very unlikely" nudge (`story.ni:693`) — no disambiguation
##     preferences in Chord.
##   * The clarifying-the-parser's-choice rule (`story.ni:3052`) — see
##     reading 2.
##
## STRUCTURAL CHOICES David may rename at will: the cloaks carry no singular
## `cloak` alias — that exact word is the old gray cloak's, and the tree's
## pins on the slides and the outfit change name the gray cloak by it (a
## `states:` pin takes one word; the bonnets made the same choice for the
## fashionable hat). A typed `cloak` still reaches the cloaks through the
## parser's own singularising, prompting against the carried gray cloak —
## the same collision the 2009 Understand lines had.
##
## "silk" and "silks" also name the tent itself (its own aka), so `x silk`
## can prompt between the tent and the garments — the same ambiguity the
## 2009 Understand lines created, answered there by the clarify rule and
## here by the parser's question.
##
## Imported from `grubbers-market.chord` after the tent room stands.
## Every finished line is Gentry's.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE BUY VERB (`story.ni:688-693`)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action buying
  grammar
    buy the target
  otherwise refuse nothing-on-sale

define phrase nothing-on-sale
  Nothing is on sale.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE SILKS' SHARED RULES
##
## Taking is the theft here — the market's steal verb is the take (the
## `wares.chord` ruling) — and Teisha's refusal is the friendship line, not
## a stallkeeper's bark. Buying always wants money (the trade is giving's
## alone — the ruling in the header), so the refusal names no entity and
## holds in every story state.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define trait silk-ware
  on the player taking
    refuse sooner-go-hungry
  end on

  on the player buying
    refuse silks-no-money
  end on
end trait

define phrase sooner-go-hungry
  You'd sooner go hungry than steal from Teisha. She's your friend.
end phrase

define phrase silks-no-money
  You'd love to, but you haven't any money.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE DISPLAY (`story.ni:3025-3027`)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the silk garments
  aka display, displays, fare, wares, merchandise, goods, teishas wares, beautiful silks, silks, garment, clothes, fabric, fabrics, rippling, shimmering
  scenery, plural
  silk-ware
  starts in Inside the Silk Tent

  Gowns, robes, capes, cloaks, and shawls — the fabric rippling with the
  slightest movement of air, shimmering with that soft, liquid glow that only
  silk has. You sigh. Dressing up like a boy is definitely *fun*, but looking
  at Teisha's wares always makes you yearn for something a little bit more...
  elegant.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE FIVE KINDS (`story.ni:3046-3057`)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the gowns
  aka gown, simple, design, designs, elegant
  scenery, plural
  silk-ware
  starts in Inside the Silk Tent

  The gowns are simple designs; not like the over-decorated things all the
  rich women wear to high-fashion parties. Just elegant and beautiful.

create the capes
  aka cape, short, waist, length, decorative, inner, lining, linings, outer, color, colors, colored, colour, colours, coloured
  scenery, plural
  silk-ware
  starts in Inside the Silk Tent

  The capes are short, waist-length, more for decoration than to actually
  keep you warm. Most of them have different-colored inner linings that
  complement the outer fabric.

## The `[first time]` tail rides an after-examining phrase — the base text
## is the description, the realization its own paragraph after it, the
## nearest shape to the source's in-description alternation. See reading 1
## in the header: carried verbatim, placement and timing David's call.

create the cloaks
  aka long, voluminous, hood, hoods
  scenery, plural
  silk-ware
  starts in Inside the Silk Tent

  The cloaks are long and voluminous. Most of them would reach down to your
  ankles and wrap completely around your shoulders, and with the hood up
  your face would be draped in shadow.

  after the player examining
    phrase cloaks-realization
  end after

define phrase cloaks-realization, first-time
  You realize suddenly that this is exactly what you need: the perfect
  disguise!
or
  The perfect disguise, if only you could afford one.
end phrase

create the shawls
  aka shawl, wrap, wraps, bright, brightly, color, colors, colored, colour, colours, coloured
  scenery, plural
  silk-ware
  starts in Inside the Silk Tent

  The shawls are just simple wraps of brightly colored silk, to cover your
  arms and shoulders.

create the robes
  aka robe, sleeve, sleeves, long, sleeved, tie, ties
  scenery, plural
  silk-ware
  starts in Inside the Silk Tent

  The robes are long-sleeved, with ties in the front.
